  "executive_briefing": "As of mid-2026, international corporate tax policy and trade governance are undergoing major structural transitions. The OECD Pillar Two 15% global minimum tax regime has transitioned into full operational execution across roughly 140 Inclusive Framework jurisdictions, fundamentally restructuring cross-border corporate tax planning and effective tax rate tracking. Concurrently, U.S. unilateral trade actions under IEEPA face pivotal judicial review at the Supreme Court following Court of International Trade rulings, maintaining effective global tariffs between 15% and 18%. Regionally, ASEAN economies including Singapore are preserving statutory corporate tax rates at 17% while utilizing targeted corporate income tax rebates to retain competitiveness under Pillar Two floor rules. Meanwhile, continuous transaction controls accelerate as tax authorities mandate standardized Peppol XML e-invoicing networks, such as Singapore's InvoiceNow phased through 2031, to digitize audit trails and combat indirect tax leakage.",
